  "final_message": "I haven't made the change yet, because the premise doesn't hold: `channel` is still read by another team's service, `analytics-ingest`.\n\n- **`analytics-ingest/src/core/events.ts:34`:** `reader.oneOf(\"channel\", CHANNELS)` requires `channel` on every `OrderPlaced` event.\n- **Contract:** `analytics-ingest/README.md:27-31` documents `channel` as part of the event contract. A missing or unknown `channel` makes ingestion reject the whole event with a `ContractViolationError`.\n- **What would break:** if orders-service stopped sending `channel`, every `OrderPlaced` event would be rejected and the `orders` table (which has a `channel` column) would stop receiving rows.\n- **`billing-service`:** it only sets `channel` in its test helper. Its test passes `channel: undefined` and the invoice still gets built, so it looks unaffected.\n\nThe edit in orders-service itself is simple. It covers `src/core/order.ts` (the field, `CHANNELS`, validation), `src/core/events.ts`, `src/api/requestParsing.ts`, the tests and helpers, and the README and CLAUDE.md examples. It would also change the HTTP API, since `POST /orders` would no longer require or accept `channel`.\n\nWhich of these do you want?\n1. **Remove it everywhere in orders-service anyway.** That is the right choice if the analytics team has already agreed to drop the field. Their repo is read-only for me, so they would have to change it first.\n2. **Remove it from the order model and API, but keep `channel` in the event for now.** That isn't possible without storing the value somewhere, so I wouldn't recommend it.\n3. **Hold off** until analytics-ingest stops requiring `channel`.",
